<h2>Acrylic vs PU Finish</h2>
<p>PU (polyurethane) finish is acrylic's closest competitor in the premium finish category — both deliver high-gloss or matte luxury surfaces, but through fundamentally different application processes with different strengths.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>🟦 Acrylic Sheet</th><th>🔴 PU Finish</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Application Method</td><td>Factory-bonded sheet</td><td>Spray-applied on site FLEXIBLE</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour Options</td><td>Manufacturer's range only</td><td>Any RAL / NCS custom colour WINNER</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Profile Doors</td><td>Flat panels only — cannot wrap curves</td><td>Coats curves, profiles, edges WINNER</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Colour Consistency</td><td>Factory-controlled — highly consistent WINNER</td><td>Dependent on spray skill</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Repairability</td><td>Full panel replacement needed</td><td>On-site touch-up possible WINNER</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Broadly similar premium range</td><td>Broadly similar premium range</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Choose <strong>PU finish</strong> when you need profiled or curved door designs, completely custom colour matching, or want the option of future on-site touch-ups. Choose <strong>acrylic</strong> when you want factory-guaranteed colour consistency across a large flat-panel installation.</p>
<p>12</p>
<h2>Acrylic vs Membrane Finish</h2>
<p>Membrane finish is a budget-tier PVC foil wrapped over MDF using heat and vacuum pressing — a fundamentally different and significantly less premium category than acrylic, but worth understanding for accurate budget comparison.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>🟦 Acrylic</th><th>⬜ Membrane</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Material</td><td>PMMA sheet 0.8–1mm PREMIUM</td><td>PVC film 0.2–0.45mm</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Heat Tolerance</td><td>Good — up to 70°C WINNER</td><td>Poor — peels near ovens</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Durability</td><td>15–20 years WINNER</td><td>5–8 years</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost</td><td>₹1,400–₹2,800 installed</td><td>₹400–₹700 installed CHEAPER</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Profile Design</td><td>Flat panels only</td><td>Wraps profiles and curves FLEXIBLE</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Membrane finish is appropriate for traditional, profile-door furniture designs on a budget. For any kitchen application near heat sources, or any project where longevity is the priority, acrylic is the correct choice over membrane without exception.</p>
<p>13</p>
<h2>Acrylic vs Glass Finish</h2>
<p>Lacquered glass is acrylic's true luxury-tier competitor — both deliver a glossy, colour-rich surface, but glass sits a clear step above acrylic in cost, weight, and perceived prestige.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Criteria</th><th>🟦 Acrylic</th><th>🪟 Glass (Lacquered)</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Appearance</td><td>Excellent — near-glass depth</td><td>Marginally superior depth and clarity WINNER</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Safety</td><td>Shatter-resistant polymer — no injury risk WINNER</td><td>Breakage risk even when toughened</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Weight</td><td>Lightweight — standard hardware adequate WINNER</td><td>Substantially heavier — needs heavy-duty hardware</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Lower — no structural hardware premium WINNER</td><td>30–60% more than acrylic equivalent</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Prestige</td><td>High</td><td>Highest — true luxury material WINNER</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>For most Indian residential projects, acrylic delivers 90% of the visual result of lacquered glass at significantly lower cost, weight, and risk. Glass is justified as a deliberate luxury upgrade in designer homes where its material weight and prestige are part of the intended experience.</p>
