<p>15</p>
<h2>Best Acrylic Colours for Home Interiors</h2>
<p>Colour selection in acrylic carries higher stakes than in most other finishes, because acrylic's depth and reflectivity genuinely amplify whatever colour you choose — making both good and poor colour decisions more visually pronounced than they would be in laminate or paint.</p>
<p>White Acrylic</p>
<p>Timeless, brightens small spaces. Shows fingerprints most readily in gloss — specify matte white for daily-use surfaces.</p>
<p>Beige / Warm Nude</p>
<p>Warm, forgiving of fingerprints, pairs beautifully with wood accents and brass hardware.</p>
<p>Grey Acrylic</p>
<p>Modern, versatile, moderate fingerprint visibility, ages well over time. The safest "statement neutral".</p>
<p>Navy Blue</p>
<p>Rich depth in gloss, dramatic statement colour. Best in matte for daily-use surfaces — gloss navy is striking but unforgiving.</p>
<p>Olive Green</p>
<p>2026's most-requested colour. Earthy and biophilic, pairs perfectly with brass hardware and stone countertops.</p>
<p>Black Acrylic</p>
<p>Maximum drama in gloss, highest fingerprint visibility of any colour. Reserve exclusively for low-contact accent surfaces.</p>
<h3>Dual-Tone Acrylic Combinations</h3>
<p>Dual-tone acrylic — pairing two complementary colours across upper and lower cabinet runs, or island versus perimeter cabinetry — is the dominant kitchen and wardrobe design strategy in premium Indian interiors for 2026. The most successful combinations pair a darker, richer lower tone (navy, olive, charcoal) with a lighter upper tone (white, ivory, soft grey).</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>Colour Selection Rule of Thumb</strong><p>For any acrylic surface with frequent hand contact, choose mid-tone or darker matte finishes over very light or very dark gloss — light gloss colours show every smudge and dust particle, while very dark gloss colours show fingerprints with the highest contrast of any colour category. Mid-tone matte colours are simply the most forgiving choice for genuine daily-life durability.</div>
<p>16</p>
<h2>Acrylic Finish Maintenance Guide</h2>
<p>Proper maintenance is what separates an acrylic installation that looks new after 10 years from one that looks worn and scratched after 2. These practices apply across both gloss and matte acrylic finishes.</p>
<h3>Daily Cleaning Practices</h3>
<ul>
  <li>✓Wipe surfaces with a dry or barely-damp microfibre cloth to remove dust and light marks before they accumulate</li>
  <li>✓Address spills, splashes, and fingerprints promptly rather than letting them set</li>
  <li>✓Always wipe in a single direction rather than circular scrubbing motions on gloss surfaces</li>
  <li>✓Dry surfaces fully after damp wiping to prevent water spotting, particularly on dark gloss colours</li>
</ul>
<h3>Weekly Maintenance Checklist</h3>
<ul>
  <li>✓Clean handles, hinges, and high-contact areas with a mild soap solution to remove accumulated skin oils</li>
  <li>✓Inspect edge-banding for any signs of lifting, particularly near sinks or steam-exposed areas</li>
  <li>✓Check hinges and drawer slides for smooth operation, tightening or lubricating as needed</li>
  <li>✓Dust upper cabinet surfaces and cornices where dust accumulates less visibly</li>
</ul>
<h3>Cleaning Products to Avoid</h3>
<h4>✅ Safe to Use</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Microfibre cloths (dry or lightly damp)</li>
  <li>Mild dish soap diluted in water</li>
  <li>Ammonia-free glass/acrylic cleaning sprays</li>
  <li>Soft cotton cloths for buffing</li>
</ul>
<h4>❌ Never Use</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Scouring pads, steel wool, or melamine sponges</li>
  <li>Ammonia-based or alcohol-based cleaners</li>
  <li>Abrasive powder cleaners of any kind</li>
  <li>Acetone, nail polish remover, or other solvents</li>
</ul>
<p>17</p>
<h2>How to Identify Good Quality Acrylic</h2>
<p>Acrylic quality varies enormously across the Indian market, and visual inspection in a showroom under controlled lighting often cannot reveal the difference between a sheet that will perform well for 15 years and one that will yellow or scratch within 2.</p>
<h3>Thickness Standards</h3>
<p>Request the exact sheet thickness specification in writing — furniture-grade acrylic sheet for shutters should be a minimum of 0.8mm, with 1mm being the more durable standard for high-traffic kitchen and wardrobe applications. Anything thinner is typically an acrylic-coated laminate being marketed under acrylic branding.</p>
<h3>Surface Gloss Quality</h3>
<p>A genuine high-gloss acrylic sheet should show a gloss reading of 90+ gloss units when tested, and visually should produce a clear, distortion-free reflection — hold a straight-edged object near the surface and check that its reflected image is not wavy or distorted.</p>
<h3>UV Protection and Edge Finishing Quality</h3>
<p>Ask specifically whether the acrylic sheet includes UV-stabilising additives. Inspect a sample panel's edge-banding closely — the seam should be smooth to touch with no detectable ridge, and the edge band colour should closely match the face colour under both daylight and artificial light. PUR hot-melt edge banding is the quality benchmark.</p>
<p>18</p>
<h2>Common Mistakes Homeowners Make with Acrylic</h2>
<p>These recurring errors account for the majority of post-installation acrylic complaints in Indian homes — nearly all of them avoidable with the right information before the order is placed.</p>
<p>1</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Choosing dark, high-gloss colours for kitchen handle areas or daily-use wardrobe doors without understanding the fingerprint maintenance burden this creates.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Reserve dark gloss for low-contact accent surfaces; specify matte finish for any surface with frequent hand contact, particularly handle zones and wardrobe doors.</p>
<p>2</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Selecting low-quality, thin acrylic-coated sheets while believing they are purchasing genuine solid acrylic sheet panels — a common showroom mislabelling issue.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Always request exact sheet thickness in writing (0.8mm–1mm for genuine solid acrylic) and ask directly whether the product is solid PMMA acrylic sheet or an acrylic-coated laminate film.</p>
<p>3</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Focusing entirely on the acrylic sheet quality while ignoring core board and edge-banding quality — the actual source of most long-term durability problems.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Specify HDHMR core for any moisture-exposed application and PUR hot-melt edge banding for all panels — these two specifications matter more for long-term performance than the acrylic sheet brand itself.</p>
<p>4</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Using abrasive cleaning materials — scouring pads, melamine sponges, harsh chemical cleaners — that permanently dull or scratch the acrylic surface within months of installation.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Use only soft microfibre cloths with mild soap or ammonia-free cleaning sprays; communicate this clearly to household help who may default to more abrasive cleaning habits.</p>
<p>5</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Specifying acrylic for every surface in a kitchen or bedroom regardless of contact frequency or visibility, driving project cost up without proportional visual benefit.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Use a hybrid approach — solid acrylic on high-visibility, low-contact statement surfaces, and laminate or matte PU on secondary or high-contact surfaces — to control cost while preserving premium visual impact where it matters most.</p>
<p>19</p>
<h2>Design Tips for Using Acrylic in Apartments and Villas</h2>
<p>The right acrylic application strategy differs meaningfully between compact apartment living and larger villa spaces.</p>
<h3>Acrylic for Small Apartments</h3>
<p>In compact 1BHK and 2BHK apartments, gloss acrylic's light-reflecting properties offer genuine functional value. Keep the colour palette lighter (white, soft grey, pale beige) to maximise the brightening effect, and concentrate the acrylic investment on one or two high-impact surfaces rather than spreading a smaller budget across every cabinet in the home.</p>
<h3>Acrylic for Luxury Villas</h3>
<p>Larger villa spaces can support bolder, darker acrylic colour choices and larger uninterrupted surface areas without the same brightness concerns. Villa kitchens and dressing rooms are well-suited to dual-tone acrylic schemes, full-height acrylic wardrobe walls, and acrylic combined with natural stone countertops for a complete luxury material palette.</p>
<h3>Combining Acrylic with Other Materials</h3>
<p><strong>With Veneer:</strong> Pairing acrylic's smooth, reflective surface with natural wood veneer is one of the most effective ways to prevent an all-acrylic space from feeling cold. Use acrylic for the main cabinet shutters with a wood veneer kickboard, cornice, or open shelving insert to introduce warmth and texture.</p>
<p><strong>With Glass:</strong> Using acrylic for the main shutter surfaces while reserving lacquered or fluted glass for select display cabinet doors allows a project to access glass's slightly superior prestige in small, strategic doses without the full cost and weight implications of an all-glass installation.</p>
<p><strong>With Stone:</strong> Acrylic cabinetry paired with natural stone or engineered quartz countertops is the dominant premium Indian kitchen design language for 2026 — acrylic's colour-rich vertical surfaces balanced with stone's natural texture and durability on the horizontal work surface.</p>
